Verified: three consecutive `karr agentname` calls return claim-odds, feel-cast, page-gear. The name is freshly minted each time and nowhere persisted.

Cmd/AgentName.pm:19 documents the idiom as:
    karr pick --claim "$(karr agentname)" --move in-progress

That is correct for the single call it shows. The trap is what it teaches by example: an agent that later runs `karr handoff ID --claim "$(karr agentname)"` gets a DIFFERENT name than the one it claimed with, so the claim does not match and continuity is lost. This bit a worker in this very session — it claimed #171 as one name and could not hand off under it.

It matters because the whole multi-agent workflow is built on claims: pick/move take a claim, handoff and the claim-timeout logic compare against it, and karr-foundation attributes stalls per claim name. A mismatch is silent — nothing errors, the card just ends up attributed to a name nobody holds.

Two directions:
 (a) Make the name stable for the duration of an agent: derive it deterministically (repo + pid/session) or persist it, so repeated calls in one run agree. Careful: it must NOT become stable across genuinely different agents on the same repo, or two agents collide on one claim.
 (b) Keep it random but make the docs carry the warning and show the only safe idiom:
        NAME=$(karr agentname)
        karr move ID in-progress --claim "$NAME"
        karr handoff ID --claim "$NAME" --note "..."
     and say plainly that the name is not recoverable once lost.

(b) is the smaller change and may well be the right one — but decide deliberately, and whichever way it goes, the POD example must stop teaching the shape that breaks. Also check the kanban-issues-karr-cli skill for the same pattern.
2026-08-15 09:31 Chose direction (b): generator stays random and stateless, docs carry the warning and show only the capture-once idiom. Rationale: every handle stable enough to survive across separate karr processes (board, git identity, host) is equally shared by concurrent agents on that board, so (a) would trade a refused mismatch for an unrefusable collision; and agentname is also legitimately used to mint several distinct names. Also corrected the ticket's premise that a lost name is unrecoverable — karr show ID prints Claimed:, karr pick prints (claimed by NAME), and check_claim's refusal names the holder; the docs now point at those.

